open-alert-base (0.24.0+oa1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The agent moves to RPi ALERT v0.24.0, unchanged from its signed
    release. 0.24 adds four dashboard settings for a managed network:
    web.bind (the address it listens on), web.hosts (more names it answers
    to), web.behindProxy (a reverse proxy's callers, and a web page from
    another site in a browser on the same computer, are never taken for
    the computer itself; a page the computer itself serves, on any port,
    still is) and web.requireLogin "all" (nothing is shown without the
    password).
  * Enterprise profile: the agent itself now listens on 127.0.0.1 only
    (web.bind, which 0.23 ignored); a reverse proxy's callers must log in
    before they see anything. The firewall lock stays, as the second
    guard: no lock, no agent.
  * Standard profile: web.behindProxy on. It changes nothing another
    computer sees; a web page from another site, in a browser on this
    computer, is no longer taken for the computer itself.
  * The profile holds these three settings where it asks for the closed
    value: each time the agent starts, the start command first puts back
    whatever the settings file, which the agent's own user can write, has
    looser. One loosened while the agent runs (rpi-alert config set) lasts
    until the next start; status, audit and sync name it, and sync
    restarts the service to put it back.
  * audit reports web.bind, web.behindProxy, web.requireLogin and
    web.hosts, and /etc/rpi-alert.conf if it is there (this package does
    not apply it).
  * sync, status and audit name the port the agent really opens: one
    below 1024 that the service has no privilege for is 8080, where the
    agent falls back. sync no longer asks for the firewall lock to be
    moved to a port the agent cannot open.
  * selftest leaves out the tests of RPi ALERT's updater and three that
    read its Raspberry Pi installer's own files, which are not installed.
  * 0.23.1+oa1 and 0.23.1+oa2 were built and tested, and never released.

 -- open-ALERT <cdomotor-g@users.noreply.github.com>  Thu, 19 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000

open-alert-base (0.23.1+oa2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The same agent, RPi ALERT v0.23.1, unchanged; the layer around it
    changed after two reviews.
  * Enterprise profile: no lock, no agent. The agent runs only while the
    firewall lock that keeps the dashboard to this computer holds; the lock
    is put back at once when the firewall tables are cleared, and the agent
    comes back by itself once it holds again.
  * The lock and the privilege for port 80 are decided from root's own
    record of the profile, not from settings the agent can write.
  * Web proxy: a setting that cannot be used is refused instead of passed
    over, and a proxy that drops connections is not hammered.
  * Settings files owned by root are handed to the agent's user; the
    installed size is counted from the files; USB serial drivers load at
    once; reinstalling with OPEN_ALERT_BASE_PROFILE is honoured.
  * 0.23.1+oa1 was a snapshot and was never released.

 -- open-ALERT <cdomotor-g@users.noreply.github.com>  Wed, 18 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000

open-alert-base (0.23.1+oa1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * First package: the RPi ALERT agent from its signed release v0.23.1,
    unchanged, as a native package for Debian 13 and Ubuntu 24.04
    (x86-64 and 64-bit ARM).
  * A locked-down service with no root helper, the standard and enterprise
    profiles, web-proxy support, and the audit, selftest and bench commands.
  * Not yet tested with a real radio stick on hardware other than a
    Raspberry Pi.

 -- open-ALERT <cdomotor-g@users.noreply.github.com>  Wed, 11 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
